00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.710
And as always, I will strive
to be as accurate as possible,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:27.130
as thorough as possible, and
yet as concise as possible.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.670
The first question today
is about adaptogens.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.920
Some of you may have
heard of adaptogens.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.770
I'm guessing many of you
have not heard of adaptogens.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.590
The strict definition of
adaptogens is still evolving.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.560
Meaning, no one really
knows what adaptogens mean

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.570
and what's included
in adaptogens

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.610
and what's excluded
from adaptogens.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.460
But the most common
definition of an adaptogen

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.742
is a compound that is typically
a supplement or a drug.

00:01:51.742 --> 00:01:53.700
Although, it could be a
behavior, if you really

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.360
think about it, that helps you
adapt to and buffer stress.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.330
So when you hear
about adaptogens,

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.700
there are three main
categories of adaptogens

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:03.820
that come to mind.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.970
The first are things that
are contained in food.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.330
So these would be
vitamins or micronutrients

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.670
that one could
easily find in food.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.230
But one would have to consume
a fairly restricted number

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.630
or type of foods in order
to obtain those things,

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.940
or consume a lot of
those foods in order

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.990
to get sufficient dosages
of those adaptogen compounds

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.060
in order to buffer stress.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.620
Some good examples
of these would

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.060
be any kind of vitamin, either
water-soluble or fat-soluble,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.530
that can adjust or reduce what
are so-called reactive oxygen

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:38.277
species.

00:02:38.277 --> 00:02:40.110
And then that's what
gives rise to this idea

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.390
that antioxidants
are good for us.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.820
Now, over the last 10 years
or so, there's been a shift.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:45.840
What shift has occurred?

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.630
Well, about 10 years
ago, you often heard

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.133
about antioxidants,
antioxidants, antioxidants

00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:52.800
and vitamins,
antioxidants in this food,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.720
this superfood, et cetera.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.120
And why were people
talking about antioxidants?

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.410
Well, just to remind you,
reactive oxygen species

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.290
are types of reactions
and molecules

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.700
that occur in cells when
cells get stressed and/or age.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.980
And antioxidants
are the compounds

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.110
that reduce those
reactive oxygen species.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:16.380
Reactive oxygen species
are bad for cells

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.270
because they tend to hinder
the function of mitochondria,

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.910
which are associated with energy
production in those cells.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.560
So what do we know for sure?

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.000
We know that as cells
get older, or as any cell

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.980
or biological
system organ tissue,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.250
et cetera, get stressed
a lot over time,

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.130
the number of reactive
oxygen species

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.770
increases in those cells
and tissues and organs.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.550
And antioxidants, which can
include certain vitamins

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.130
but also some
micronutrients, are

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.868
effective in reducing those
reactive oxygen species.

00:03:46.868 --> 00:03:48.660
Now, what's occurred
over the last 10 years

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.820
is that we know that
reactive oxygen species are

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:54.660
a major source of depleting
cellular function by way

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.340
of depleting
mitochondrial function.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.340
But they are just one
of many mechanisms

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.260
that can deplete
cellular function

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.410
mitochondrial function.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.250
So nowadays, you'll hear
about reactive oxygen species

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.830
and antioxidants, but not
as much as you used to.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.980
Now, you hear a lot more
about inflammatory responses

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.350
and inflammatory cytokines
also being an issue.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.850
And the truth is all of
these things are an issue.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.779
So going back to this
question about adaptogens,

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:21.180
adaptogens include
these three categories.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.430
I've told you the first,
which are the vitamins

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:27.000
and micronutrients that
are contained in food that

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.490
can reduce reactive
oxygen species

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.990
and other aspects
of cell or stress,

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.540
such as inflammatory cytokines.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.820
What are some of those
things that occur in foods?

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.860
Well, in order to
answer that, let's

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.020
just think about what
sorts of foods, themselves,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.690
can act as adaptogens.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:47.115
It's commonly held that the
dark, leafy greens type foods,

00:04:47.115 --> 00:04:48.490
for those of you
that eat plants.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.460
And I think the majority of
people out there do eat plants.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.230
I know that the
carnivore diet and lion

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.770
diet and some other diets
tend to exclude plants,

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.843
and we'll address that
briefly at some point

00:04:58.843 --> 00:04:59.760
in today's discussion.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.280
But dark, leafy greens
are known to contain

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.430
a number of compounds
in the form of vitamins

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.000
and micronutrients that are
very effective in reducing

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.427
reactive oxygen species
and inflammatory cytokines.

00:05:12.427 --> 00:05:14.010
So if you're somebody
who's interested

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.700
in adaptogens and adaptogenic
processes, reducing

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.460
stress and buffering stress,
which, of course, has

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.890
its role in buffering
daily stress in order

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.510
to help you sleep
better to improve

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.760
cellular function for
longevity sports performance,

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.740
cognitive performance,
that is all good

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:29.820
and it makes sense
why people would

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:30.987
be interested in adaptogens.

00:05:30.987 --> 00:05:33.930
But remember that the
two main adaptogens

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.630
that you should think
to first are going

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.130
to be behaviors and nutrition.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.095
I started with
nutrition on purpose.

00:05:40.095 --> 00:05:42.220
As I mentioned, we'll get
to behaviors in a moment.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:44.250
So if you're interested
in adaptogens at all,

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.980
I highly recommend that
you include at least two

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.380
to four servings of
dark, leafy greens

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.000
and/or cruciferous
vegetables per day.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.850
I think that would be
highly advantageous.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:57.060
And just be aware that
excessively heating dark,

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.800
leafy greens or
cruciferous vegetables

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.960
can actually destroy
the very nutrients

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.510
and micronutrients that
act in an adaptogenic way.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.700
That does not mean that you
need to eat raw broccoli

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.160
or cauliflower, just the thought
of that makes me nauseous,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.202
it's very hard to digest.

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:12.300
Some people might like that
or can digest it more easily

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.000
than others.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.840
So it's perfectly fine
to cook your cruciferous

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.400
vegetables and
dark leafy greens,

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.050
but you don't want
to overcook them.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.690
What's overcooking and
what's undercooking?

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.413
There's no strict cutoff
in terms of temperature.

00:06:24.413 --> 00:06:25.830
But basically,
what the literature

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.950
says is that if you heat
vegetables to the point

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.860
where the colored
fluid is leaching out

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:36.160
of them into a broth
type, into water

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.340
or whatever fluid surrounds
them, well, then you

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.920
would be well off to ingest
that fluid as well because it

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.850
contained in the water or
the fluid that's leaching out

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.485
from the cruciferous vegetables
or from the dark leafy greens,

00:06:47.485 --> 00:06:49.860
are going to be a lot of those
very adaptogenic molecules

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.570
that you're interested
in the first place.

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:51.970
OK.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:53.160
So I probably
surprised some people

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.990
by starting off my answer
to the question of "What

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:55.860
are adaptogens?

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.020
Are they worth thinking
about in pursuing?

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.703
And if so, how can I get
them?" by talking about food.

00:07:00.703 --> 00:07:03.120
But I think it is important
to understand that you can get

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.470
a lot of adaptogens from food.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.270
And indeed, some of
the best adaptogens

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.940
do come from dark leafy greens
and cruciferous vegetables.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.190
So I'd be remiss if I
didn't mention that.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.680
The other two
categories of adaptogens

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.620
are going to be supplement-based
adaptogens and then

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:17.910
behavioral adaptogens.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.310
Again, here adaptogen
defined as anything

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:24.060
that can buffer stress in a
substantial or meaningful way

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.670
in order to support
cellular health, organ

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:30.820
health, and overall daily
living and functioning,

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.130
including sleep and
performance in mental health.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.343
So the second
category of adaptogens

00:07:35.343 --> 00:07:36.510
are going to be supplements.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:38.590
And here, again, I just
want to take a step back,

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:40.110
make sure that we
are clear about

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:43.170
our operational definition
about what a supplement is.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:47.280
We had an episode all about
how to design a rational guide

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.800
to supplementation,
which included,

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:51.990
for example, the idea
that for some people,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.150
the optimal dosage
of many supplements

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:55.007
is going to be zero.

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:57.340
And for other people, the
dosage will be something else.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.600
But to really pinpoint the
key message from that episode

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:04.410
that I'd like to reiterate
now, but a key message

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.600
from that episode that
I'd like to reiterate now

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.180
is that many people
think of supplements

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.850
as just vitamin supplements.

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:13.782
And for that
reason, you'll often

00:08:13.782 --> 00:08:15.990
hear the argument, "Oh,
well, aren't supplements just

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:16.873
expensive urine.

00:08:16.873 --> 00:08:18.540
Couldn't you get all
of that from food?"

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.600
Aren't you just urinating out
all the water-soluble vitamins

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.322
and maybe even
storing excess amounts

00:08:23.322 --> 00:08:25.530
of the fat-soluble vitamins
in a way that's unhealthy

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:26.770
or not cost-effective?"

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:28.350
And so on.

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.210
When we talk about
supplements, yes, it

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.250
can include vitamin supplements.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.110
However, there
are many compounds

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.840
that we would describe as
supplements that are not

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.539
vitamin supplements, and that
you could not obtain from food

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:42.270
or that you could
never obtain from food

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.220
in sufficient enough
qualities to have

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:47.490
a robust positive biological
effect without consuming

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:51.030
an enormous number of calories
or overriding your gut

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:51.840
mechanically.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.023
For instance, if there
were, say, a herb, and we'll

00:08:57.023 --> 00:08:58.440
talk about such
herbs in a moment,

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.020
that contained an
effective adaptogen,

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:03.450
but you would never
want to eat the plant

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.660
itself or include that
herb in any kind of recipe,

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.600
well then, chances
are this herb, which

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.840
we'll define in a moment,
is not a vitamin supplement.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.990
It is probably not even best
thought of as a supplement,

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.430
it's best thought
of as a compound

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.220
that sold over the counter,
much like a prescription drug.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.690
Although, it's not prescription.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.350
It doesn't require a
prescription to get it.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:25.058
So there are a lot of
things like that we

00:09:25.058 --> 00:09:27.420
include under the umbrella
of this word supplements.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:29.953
And unfortunately, because
of that, a lot of people

00:09:29.953 --> 00:09:31.620
think, "Oh, you don't
need supplements."

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.990
And, of course, you don't
need supplements per se,

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.570
but many people do derive
tremendous benefit from them.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.220
In the context of
adaptogens, there

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.900
are two or three
in particular that

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:42.690
can be very beneficial
for buffering the stress

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.730
response, especially,
over short periods of time

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.630
of about two to three weeks.

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:47.970
So when would you use these?

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.640
Well, for instance, if you are
in a particularly stressful

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.590
mode of life, either because of
family or relational or school

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:58.290
or work demands or
new kid in the house

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:00.720
or-- any number of different
things, or you've been ill

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.700
or you're recovering
from injury,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.130
taking an adaptogen in
the form of a supplement

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:08.490
can actually be very useful
for buffering this hormone

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.410
and the general system
is associated with,

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:11.550
called cortisol.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:13.860
It's very healthy
to have high levels

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.680
of cortisol early in the day,
shortly after you wake up.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.320
And then that ought to taper
off toward the afternoon

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:19.860
and evening.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.930
However, if cortisol
is chronically

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.330
elevated throughout the day,
or if that peak in cortisol

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.247
is arriving too late
in the day, that

00:10:26.247 --> 00:10:28.080
is known to be associated
with mental health

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.205
and physical health issues.

00:10:29.205 --> 00:10:31.440
Has been shown by labs at
Stanford and elsewhere.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.410
It's been shown in animal
models and in humans.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.930
Talked many times before, and
I'll just remind you again.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:41.760
One of the best ways to
restrict that cortisol peak

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.580
to the early part of the day
is to get morning sunlight

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:46.380
in your eyes as soon as you can.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:47.880
Once the sun is up,
get outside face

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.005
in the direction of the
sun, even on overcast days.

00:10:50.005 --> 00:10:52.740
Don't wear sunglasses, look
at it for five to 30 minutes.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.870
Definitely blink so you
don't damage your eyes,

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:56.730
so on and so forth.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.128
Why five minutes or 30 minutes?

00:10:58.128 --> 00:11:00.420
Well, five minutes on a clear
day should be sufficient,

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.440
longer would be fine.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.510
Again, blink so that
you protect your eyes.

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:04.320
Blink as needed.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.060
Face in the general
direction of the sun.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:10.410
On days when you have a lot of
overcast or it's really dark,

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.910
dense cloud cover,
well, then, you'd

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.060
want to be outside longer.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:15.370
And if you don't have access to
sunlight for whatever reason,

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:16.787
then you want to
do the same thing

00:11:16.787 --> 00:11:19.770
with bright artificial lights
indoors, either so-called SAD

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:20.550
lamp or otherwise.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:22.830
That's a great way to
restrict that cortisol peak

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:24.228
to early in the day.

00:11:24.228 --> 00:11:25.770
But even if you're
doing that, if you

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:27.880
have a stressful life
for whatever reason,

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.610
even if you're getting that
morning sunlight, which I hope

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:31.193
you are, you're
getting your exercise,

00:11:31.193 --> 00:11:34.470
you're trying to sleep
better and more as we all

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:37.470
should most of the
time, well, then you

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:41.190
may be somebody who
wants to take a adaptogen

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:42.960
in the form of a supplement.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:44.700
And the three
supplements that can

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.920
be very effective in
buffering cortisol

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:51.660
are ashwagandha, which I'll
talk about first, lion's mane

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:52.710
and chaga.

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:56.505
Lion's mane and chaga
are in the fungi group,

00:11:56.505 --> 00:11:57.630
so they count as mushrooms.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:58.950
They are not
psychedelic mushrooms.

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:00.510
Let's talk about
ashwagandha first.

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:02.070
Ashwagandha is at
the top of the list

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:05.580
because it is indeed a
very potent adaptogen.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:06.600
How can I say that?

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:08.370
Well, there are a
number of studies

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.740
now, including
several excellent ones

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:14.340
in humans, that
report that taking

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:19.170
two doses of 300 milligrams
of ashwagandha per day

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.630
can, very dramatically,
buffer cortisol.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.120
So this is something
that you would

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:28.182
have a near-impossible
time accessing from food.

00:12:28.182 --> 00:12:29.640
I can't imagine
that, unless you're

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:32.425
cooking with the very
sources of ashwagandha

00:12:32.425 --> 00:12:34.050
and extracting exact
amounts that you'd

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:37.625
be able to get this in any other
form except supplement form.

00:12:37.625 --> 00:12:39.750
So here I'm going to just
briefly reference a paper

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:43.740
and we can provide a reference
link to this, that is.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.930
This is a paper
from 2012 that's had

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:48.347
a lot of excellent follow-up
papers that support it.

00:12:48.347 --> 00:12:50.430
The title of the paper is
A prospective randomized

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.430
double-blind placebo-controlled
study of safety and efficacy

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:55.050
of high concentration
of full spectrum

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:57.090
extract of ashwagandha
root in reducing

00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:59.760
stress and anxiety in adults.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.080
And it's a really nice study.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:03.542
Not a huge subject pool,
but both men and women, and

00:13:03.542 --> 00:13:05.250
has carried out for
long enough that they

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.140
got to see some really
interesting results.

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:10.620
And I think the most
interesting result

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:15.540
is that taking 300 milligrams
of ashwagandha twice a day

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.340
led to enormous--

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:21.120
I mean, just enormous
changes in serum cortisol.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.760
The statistical significance
that they observed in the study

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:28.320
was really fantastic,
fantastically high

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:29.760
statistical significance.

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:33.990
They saw the effects of
ashwagandha on day 15,

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:38.130
having initiated the ashwagandha
consumption on day one,

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:40.350
of course, day 30 and day 45.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:42.570
And again, this was
dramatic reductions

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:45.540
in stress as
perceived by people.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:47.760
So subjective stress
and cortisol levels.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.310
So ashwagandha is very
potent at reducing cortisol.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.860
How would you
recapitulate this if you

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:54.780
wanted to use ashwagandha
to buffer stress?

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:56.903
Well, a couple of key points.

00:13:56.903 --> 00:13:59.070
I mentioned earlier that
you want your cortisol peak

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:01.610
to come earlier in the day.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:03.440
Therefore, you would
not want to buffer

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:04.710
cortisol early in the day.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:07.730
In fact, cortisol
peaking early in the day

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:13.340
provides an anti-inflammatory
immune-supporting, focus

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:16.010
and mood supporting
effect all day long.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:18.110
So I would recommend
that people take

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:22.160
their first dose of ashwagandha
anywhere from 250 to 300

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:22.880
milligrams.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:26.450
Sometime in the early afternoon
and again in the evening,

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:29.360
as opposed to taking a morning
dose in an afternoon dose.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.180
Also, if you're somebody
who's exercising

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:35.390
for sake of trying to induce
adaptations like hypertrophy,

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:36.920
the growth of
muscles or strength,

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:38.690
or improve your
endurance in any way,

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:41.420
muscular endurance or more
traditional cardiovascular

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:45.950
endurance, then I recommend that
you not take your ashwagandha

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:49.250
prior to exercise because part
of the adaptation response

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:52.970
is triggered by increases
in cortisol during exercise.

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:56.720
Sort of in the same way
that some of the best

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:59.210
adaptations to exercise are
reductions in blood pressure

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:00.380
and resting heart rate.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:03.740
And those are stimulated by
increases in blood pressure

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:05.990
and increases in heart
rate during exercise.

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:07.920
That's just how these
biological systems work.

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.840
So the takeaway
is pretty simple.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.650
If you're interested in using
ashwagandha as an adaptogen,

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:14.300
I would restrict it
to later in the day

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.280
if you can and not
before exercise.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:20.060
Divide it into two doses
of 250 to 300 milligrams.

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:22.280
That's what this
paper and other papers

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.930
like it seem to indicate.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:27.217
And then a very important
final point about ashwagandha,

00:15:27.217 --> 00:15:29.300
which is that if you're
going to take ashwagandha,

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:32.907
I recommend not taking it for
longer than a month-and-a-half

00:15:32.907 --> 00:15:33.990
as they did in this study.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:35.990
In fact, I would
suggest that you only

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:40.880
take ashwagandha around periods
of moderate to extreme stress.

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:42.380
What's moderate,
what's extreme is

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:44.390
going to depend on what
you're going through.

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:47.270
Only how much stress and
life events you can tolerate.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:49.580
So if you've had trouble
sleeping and that's

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:50.450
unusual for you.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:53.360
Or, you're dealing with a very
difficult life circumstances

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:57.050
or excessive work demand, or a
new kid, as I mentioned before.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:59.390
Well, then buffering
stress with ashwagandha,

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:01.760
buffering cortisol in
the afternoon and evening

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.240
can be very beneficial for you.

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.980
But then I would say after
about 30 days maximum,

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:09.630
I would take at least
two to four weeks off.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:11.630
Two weeks is probably
enough, but four weeks off

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:15.530
because you don't want to
chronically buffer cortisol.

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:16.850
It's just not a good idea.

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:18.650
But that said, I
think ashwagandha

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.050
is a very powerful
adaptogen. I would

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:23.810
place at the top of the list
of supplement-based adaptogens.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:25.910
But keep in mind
that even if you're

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:27.410
taking a supplement-based
adaptogen,

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:31.790
that's no reason to abandon the
nutrition and behavioral type

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:34.100
adaptogenic effects that you
can create through eating

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:35.720
dark leafy greens,
cruciferous vegetables,

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.470
then we'll talk about the
behaviors in a moment.

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:40.050
The other two supplement-based
adaptogens, as I mentioned,

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:42.110
are lion's mane
mushroom and chaga.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:45.380
And I get asked a lot about
lion's mane and chaga for sake

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:50.900
of their purported roles in
acting as nootropics as quote

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:52.340
unquote "smart drugs."

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:56.270
There are fewer data
on the beneficial roles

00:16:56.270 --> 00:17:00.930
of lion's mane and chaga for
sake of nootropic effects--

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:03.260
we'll do an entire episode
on nootropics at some point.

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:05.750
But there have been
a few studies showing

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:08.000
that lion's made in
supplementation and chaga

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.430
supplementation
can improve memory,

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:12.780
and maybe even
divergent thinking

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:15.109
associated with creativity
and things of that sort.

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:17.720
Again, these are not
psychedelic mushrooms.

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:22.609
That said, there are good data
showing that 1,000 milligrams,

00:17:22.609 --> 00:17:28.069
that is 1 gram, of lion's
mane per day and/or--

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:30.470
I will talk about the
end/or portion in a moment,

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:36.350
and/or chaga mushroom at 500
to 1,500 milligrams per day

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:42.130
can act as adaptogens in,
again, reducing cortisol.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:45.550
But also and mainly reducing
some of the anti-inflammatory

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:48.580
cytokines that are known to
circulate in high abundance

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:51.130
when you're under a lot of
psychological and/or physical

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:51.730
stress.

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:55.340
Things like interleukin 6
and some related molecules.

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:56.950
So here's what I
would recommend.

00:17:56.950 --> 00:17:59.710
If you are interested
in exploring adaptogens,

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:01.570
I'm a big fan, as
some of you probably

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:03.838
know, if you heard that
episode on rational guide

00:18:03.838 --> 00:18:04.630
to supplementation.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:08.470
I'm a big fan of mainly
focusing on taking supplements

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.420
in single-ingredient
formulations

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:14.210
so that you can figure out
what dosages are best for you,

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:17.260
and so that you can toggle in
and out those adaptogens as

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:17.900
needed.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:20.110
So I, of course,
am a fan of taking

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:22.030
certain blends and mixes,
the one that we talk

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:24.292
about a lot on this
podcast and we have been

00:18:24.292 --> 00:18:26.500
a sponsor from the beginning,
I've taken for a decade

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:28.600
now long before I
ever had a podcast,

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.060
is Athletic Greens, which
some of you might note

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:32.590
does contain some ashwagandha.

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:34.180
Although, the levels
of ashwagandha

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:35.722
that are contained
in Athletic Greens

00:18:35.722 --> 00:18:39.010
are low enough that I don't see
any issue with taking Athletic

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:42.640
Greens consistently, day to
day, every day, because you're

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.460
not getting anywhere near
that 600-milligram dosage.

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:47.350
But the idea is that
if you were going

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:51.550
to take any adaptogen for
sake of buffering stress

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:55.030
over the short term, say for a
week or two weeks or a month,

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:57.220
and then taking that
recommended time off,

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:59.080
I would start with ashwagandha.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.050
And then if you feel you
need something else to buffer

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:03.552
stress, keeping in
mind, of course,

00:19:03.552 --> 00:19:06.010
that you're doing the behavioral
and the nutritional things

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:06.910
to buffer stress as well.

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:08.440
You can never
abandon those, right?

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.020
Well, then I would suggest
adding 1,000 milligrams

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:13.840
or 1,000 milligrams
of chaga per day

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:18.197
and seeing how that further
benefits your system in terms

00:19:18.197 --> 00:19:19.030
of buffering stress.

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:21.310
How would you measure if
your stress is being reduced?

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:23.410
Well, you're going to be
sleeping better at night.

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:25.368
You're going to feel
subjectively better, lower

00:19:25.368 --> 00:19:26.450
levels of anxiety.

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:28.783
All the things that are
measured in the types of studies

00:19:28.783 --> 00:19:30.110
I described before.

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:32.560
Now, of course, there's
nothing preventing you

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:34.760
from taking 600
milligrams of ashwagandha,

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:36.820
a gram of lion's mane,
and a gram of chaga.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:38.827
I know some people
like to just kind of go

00:19:38.827 --> 00:19:39.910
full tilt into everything.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:44.170
But I am a big believer in
really trying to isolate which

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:46.960
supplements and molecules work
best for you and which ones

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:47.710
don't.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:51.250
Do you need to cycle on and
off lion's mane and chaga?

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.500
I'm not aware of any
data showing that you do.

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:55.720
If, however, you're
taking them every day,

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:59.410
I recommend that you cycle off
them after a period of 30 days

00:19:59.410 --> 00:19:59.950
or so.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:01.630
And I want to be very
clear about this,

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:03.940
just because I said cycle
off after a period of 30 days

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:06.310
or so does not mean
that you can't take them

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:07.587
for a shorter period of time.

00:20:07.587 --> 00:20:09.670
So for instance, if you
know that you're coming up

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:12.940
on a big week of
stress, well, then you

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:18.190
could take ashwagandha and/or
lion's mane and/or chaga

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:20.800
for that week or just that
week or heading into that week

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:22.060
or in the following
week and then stop.

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:23.710
There's no reason why you
couldn't take them even just

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:24.310
for one day.

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:27.460
Although, the effects tend to
be a bit cumulative, at least,

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:30.587
when we're talking
about buffering anxiety.

00:20:30.587 --> 00:20:32.170
In terms of buffering
cortisol, that's

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:34.310
a very potent effect
that as far as we know,

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:35.740
is going to take
place on day one.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:38.860
Again, keep that cortisol
buffering effect away

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:40.180
from exercise.

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.310
At least, don't take
it before exercise.

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:45.498
And try and buffer your cortisol
in the afternoon and evening,

00:20:45.498 --> 00:20:48.040
and this is assuming that you're
working a conventional shift

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:49.780
and you're not up all
night and sleeping

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:52.270
all day for sake of shift work.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:53.920
So hopefully, that
clarifies things

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:55.390
about what adaptogens are.

00:20:55.390 --> 00:20:57.670
In fact, I never actually
read the specific question,

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:00.888
but I think I've touched
on a number of issues

00:21:00.888 --> 00:21:02.680
that were related to
the specific question.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:04.805
And then I'll answer the
last portion of the answer

00:21:04.805 --> 00:21:06.520
to this question
in a moment, as it

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:10.395
relates to behavioral tools
that can act as adaptogens.

00:21:10.395 --> 00:21:11.770
The question,
itself, was there's

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:14.302
a lot of mixed information
out there about adaptogens

00:21:14.302 --> 00:21:15.010
like ashwagandha.

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:16.780
And I think that relates to
what I said earlier, which

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:18.640
is that the definition
of an adaptogen

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:23.543
has not really been cemented
in various communities.

00:21:23.543 --> 00:21:25.210
It's different in
different communities,

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:28.750
and it's generally used
as a matter of convenience

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:30.920
rather than really strictly
defining what it is.

00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:35.320
And hopefully, we've defined it
accurately and broadly enough

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:38.092
today as something
that buffers stress.

00:21:38.092 --> 00:21:39.550
The second part of
the question was

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:41.800
what does the scientific
evidence say about adaptogens

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:44.210
and their ability to mediate
body stress response?

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:46.090
They say quite a
lot, and they say

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:49.390
that the stress response can
be buffered substantially

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:51.190
by certain adaptogens.

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:55.540
Mainly, dark leafy greens,
cruciferous vegetables,

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:57.700
ashwagandha, lion's
mane, and chaga,

00:21:57.700 --> 00:21:59.440
and of course, all
the behavioral things

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:01.340
that are critical that I
will list off in a moment.

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:02.830
And then the third
portion of the question

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.330
is, is there any
solid evidence that

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:07.240
has an effect on
neurotransmitters or the HPA,

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:10.420
which is part of the
stress modulation access?

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:13.930
The best evidence is that
adaptogens can reduce cortisol

00:22:13.930 --> 00:22:15.190
itself.

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:18.850
There is very little evidence
that adaptogens can directly

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:22.550
modulate neurotransmitters or
neuromodulators like dopamine,

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:23.590
serotonin, et cetera.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:26.500
But by adjusting the timing
and levels of cortisol,

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:29.380
especially in the
afternoon and evening,

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:32.860
that is going to have
indirect effects on levels

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:36.160
of dopamine, norepinephrine,
et cetera, and serotonin.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:37.640
But not direct effects.

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:41.233
So the general contour that
makes for an ideal diurnal

00:22:41.233 --> 00:22:42.900
schedule-- you heard
of nocturnal, well,

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:45.270
the opposite that is diurnal,
being awake during the daytime

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:46.103
and asleep at night.

00:22:46.103 --> 00:22:48.720
The ideal kind of landscape
of neurotransmitters

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:52.590
is higher levels of dopamine,
norepinephrine, and epinephrine

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:54.450
in the early part of
the day and cortisol,

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:56.830
so-called catecholamines,
dopamine, norepinephrine,

00:22:56.830 --> 00:22:57.750
and epinephrine.

00:22:57.750 --> 00:23:01.110
And high levels of cortisol
early in the day as

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:05.010
directed by sunlight, exercise,
caffeine, hydration, movement,

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:07.290
all that stuff, being
awake and busy and outside

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:09.360
or indoors with bright
lights and moving

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:10.890
about in the early
part of the day

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.392
and into the early afternoon,
that's the best possible way

00:23:13.392 --> 00:23:14.850
that we are aware
of to try and get

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:16.650
those catecholamines
released at the highest

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:18.150
levels in the early
part of the day.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.898
And then the ideal
contour of a 24-hour cycle

00:23:20.898 --> 00:23:22.440
will be in the later
half of the day.

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:25.110
The evening and nighttime,
you have higher levels

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:27.870
of things like serotonin
the GABAergic system,

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:30.360
all the things that are somewhat
sedative in preparing you

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:34.650
for sleep and lower levels of
the catecholamines and cortisol

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:35.890
as I described before.

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:37.470
So to directly
answer the question,

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:39.240
"Is there any evidence
that adaptogens can

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.220
alter your neurotransmitters?"

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:43.440
Yes, but only indirectly.

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:46.530
And yet, that indirect
control over neurotransmitters

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:48.670
is substantial and is important.

00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:50.993
And if you do what I described,
such as getting morning

00:23:50.993 --> 00:23:53.410
sunlight and ideally, you'd
get a little bit of deliberate

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:55.118
cold water exposure,
by the way, to boost

00:23:55.118 --> 00:23:57.630
adrenaline and
norepinephrine and dopamine,

00:23:57.630 --> 00:23:59.460
those catecholamines
early in the day.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:01.467
So quick one-minute
cold shower, even

00:24:01.467 --> 00:24:04.050
or a three-minute cold shower,
or if you have access to an ice

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:06.037
bath early in the day,
plus some sunlight,

00:24:06.037 --> 00:24:07.620
doesn't matter which
one you do first,

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:09.180
doing that early in
the day is really

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:12.510
going to create that peak of
cortisol, dopamine, epinephrine

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:14.100
early in the day.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.220
I can't emphasize how
beneficial all of that can be.

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:19.288
And exercise, if you can,
early end of the day.

00:24:19.288 --> 00:24:21.330
Some people can't exercise
till later in the day.

00:24:21.330 --> 00:24:23.460
I'd rather see people
exercise later in the day

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:25.230
than not at all,
provided it does not

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:27.503
disrupt their nighttime
sleep, which of course, sleep

00:24:27.503 --> 00:24:29.670
is the foundation of mental
health, physical health,

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:30.580
and performance.

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:33.122
So yes, there is modulation
of neurotransmitters,

00:24:33.122 --> 00:24:35.580
but most of those are downstream
of the effects on cortisol

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:36.830
that we've been talking about.

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:39.270
So we've defined
nutritional adaptogens,

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:42.030
supplement-based adaptogens--
although, I don't really

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:43.405
like the word
supplements anymore

00:24:43.405 --> 00:24:45.322
unless we're talking
about vitamin supplements

00:24:45.322 --> 00:24:46.870
for reasons we
talked about earlier.

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:48.330
And then there's
the third category

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:50.580
of adaptogens, which are the
behavioral tools that you

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:52.890
can use to buffer
stress, which qualifies

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:55.860
those as an adaptogen. I think
it's really important that we

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:58.180
always keep in mind that
yes, there are supplements,

00:24:58.180 --> 00:24:59.650
yes, there are prescription
drugs out there.

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:01.380
In fact, there are
prescription drugs

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:05.160
that you can get from a
doctor that will potently

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:06.900
zero out your cortisol.

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:09.330
But most doctors are very
reluctant to prescribe

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:11.520
those drugs because
cortisol provides

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:14.970
a very important and functional
role early in the day.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:18.030
Behaviors are very effective
at reducing cortisol.

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:20.490
What are the most effective
behaviors to reduce cortisol?

00:25:20.490 --> 00:25:24.270
Well, we talked about,
one, to restrict cortisol

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:27.060
the early part of the day, which
is viewing morning sunlight.

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:29.900
But how would you buffer
cortisol in the late afternoon?

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:31.650
It's going to be all
the things associated

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:32.880
with reducing stress.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:36.570
For instance, 10
minutes or even--

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:38.610
my laboratory and other
laboratories have shown

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.280
is even five minutes
a day of just what

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:43.447
would be called mindfulness
meditation, very simple.

00:25:43.447 --> 00:25:45.030
You don't need to
overcomplicate this.

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:47.130
You could use a great app
like the Waking Up app

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:48.660
or another app of the sort.

00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:50.363
Or, you can simply
sit down, eyes

00:25:50.363 --> 00:25:51.780
closed, breathe
through your nose,

00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:53.130
and just concentrate
on your breathing.

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:54.810
Every time your mind
drifts to something else,

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:56.160
bring it back to your breathing.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:58.380
That's shown to reduce stress.

00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:00.820
You could do a five-minute
deliberate breath work

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:01.320
practice.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:05.160
My laboratory has published
some work related to that.

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:07.530
The breath work practice
could be any number of things.

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:09.180
The two that I
recommend the most

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:11.925
would be double inhale,
followed by a full exhale,

00:26:11.925 --> 00:26:13.800
and then repeat for a
period of five minutes.

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:16.350
Known to substantially
reduce anxiety, stress,

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:18.240
and the various
physiological systems

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:19.500
associated with arousal.

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:21.210
You could also
use box breathing,

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.880
inhale, hold, exhale,
hold for equal durations

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:26.010
for a period of five
minutes, will substantially

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:26.940
reduce stress.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:29.460
I'm a big fan, as many of
you know, of yoga nidra,

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:30.683
which involves no movement.

00:26:30.683 --> 00:26:32.850
It involves just lying
there, listening to a script.

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:35.430
Lots of yoga or scripts
available online.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:38.350
If you're not interested in the
intentions and other things,

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:40.710
including yoga nidra,
you can buffer stress

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:44.070
using an adaptogen like
NSDR, non-sleep deep breast.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:46.770
If you're interested in
trying these sorts of things.

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:50.560
There's a NSDR protocol
that's 10 minutes long.

00:26:50.560 --> 00:26:53.790
Just put my name,
Huberman, and NSDR

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:55.560
into the search
browser on YouTube.

00:26:55.560 --> 00:27:00.060
And Virtusan is provided in
NSDR that's completely zero cost

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.160
and works very well
for reducing stress.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:04.080
It will also help
teach your system

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:07.080
and teach you how to learn to
fall asleep better at night.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:10.500
So any of those practices, five
to 10-minute breathing practice

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:14.940
or meditation, or
a NSDR yoga nidra.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.280
If you can do longer 20 or
30 minutes in the afternoon,

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:21.250
that's known to buffer
cortisol substantially as well.

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:23.520
Any time you're encountering
stress in real time,

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:25.857
I highly recommend
a tool over and over

00:27:25.857 --> 00:27:26.940
because it's so effective.

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:29.460
The fastest way we to
buffer stress and calm down

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:31.350
is the so-called
physiological sigh,

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:34.710
big Inhale through the nose till
your lungs are empty, but then

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:38.160
sneak in a little bit
more air by second inhale,

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:39.540
maximally inflate
the lungs, then

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:41.620
a long exhale until
you're lungs are empty.

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:44.110
One to three of those
will reduce your stress

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:45.160
substantially.

00:27:45.160 --> 00:27:47.890
Over time that should
reduce, that is, buffer

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:49.597
your cortisol acting
as an adaptogen.

00:27:49.597 --> 00:27:50.680
There are a lot of things.

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:52.360
You can take a hot bath,
you could take a hot shower,

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:53.950
you can listen to
some pleasant music.

00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:56.470
Anything that reduces
your stress, technically,

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:59.620
is an adaptogen. So I hope
I've thoroughly answered

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:00.753
your question by yours--

00:28:00.753 --> 00:28:03.170
I mean, of course, this answer
is going out to all of you.

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:06.070
This is a question that was
asked by Justine Bevilacqua.

00:28:06.070 --> 00:28:07.987
I hope I pronounced
that correctly, Justine.

00:28:07.987 --> 00:28:09.320
And thank you for that question.

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:10.060
I think there are
a lot of people

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:11.320
interested in adaptogens.

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:15.130
So now, you can use nutrition
such as cruciferous vegetables,

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:16.600
dark leafy greens.

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:18.430
And I should also
mention, if you're not

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:21.640
ingesting enough
calories each day, well,

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:24.790
then you are going to be
in a mild mode of stress.

00:28:24.790 --> 00:28:27.820
That's not to say that some
people shouldn't take in fewer

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:29.820
calories than they burn
in order to lose weight,

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:31.945
some people really need to
do that for their health

00:28:31.945 --> 00:28:33.227
or for whatever other reason.

00:28:33.227 --> 00:28:34.810
But if you restrict
calories too much,

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:37.490
you are going to
increase cortisol output.

00:28:37.490 --> 00:28:38.810
So keep that in mind.

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:43.100
So ingest sufficient calories
for you and for your goals.

00:28:43.100 --> 00:28:46.090
Aim to get dark leafy greens
and cruciferous vegetables,

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:47.770
don't overcook them.

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:49.390
If you want to
explore supplements,

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:51.490
the best supplements
to act as adaptogens

00:28:51.490 --> 00:28:54.700
are going to be ashwagandha,
lion's mane, and chaga.

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:58.420
One or two or three of
those-- although, if you're

00:28:58.420 --> 00:29:01.420
going to pick one, I'd recommend
ashwagandha, 600 milligrams

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:03.290
per day, taken in the
later-half of the day.

00:29:03.290 --> 00:29:04.990
And then there are
the behavioral tools

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:07.840
that we just talked about now,
which are anything that reduces

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.450
stress can reduce cortisol.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:13.390
And in doing so, are
technically adaptogens.

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:15.190
If you want to know
more behavioral tools

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:16.810
and other tools for
adjusting stress

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:18.280
and learn more
about adaptogens, we

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:20.800
did a whole episode
called Mastering Stress.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:22.960
So you can look to
that-- that episode also

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:26.390
pretty clearly defines, I like
to think, what short-term,

00:29:26.390 --> 00:29:28.348
medium-term, and long-term
stress really are.

00:29:28.348 --> 00:29:29.890
Keep in mind, stress
is part of life.

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:32.015
Learning how to work with
it, how to dance with it,

00:29:32.015 --> 00:29:33.490
how to buffer it is terrific.

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:36.260
But zeroing out cortisol
is not the goal.

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:39.970
The goal is to learn to modulate
and control your cortisol,

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:42.310
and that's really what
adaptogens are all about.

